{"slug": "duck-and-cover", "synopsis": "This 1951 civil defense film teaches children how to respond to an atomic-bomb warning or a sudden flash. An animated character, Bert the Turtle, withdraws into his shell when danger appears, while children demonstrate how to duck, cover their faces and necks, and shelter under desks or tables. The film explains that an atomic explosion can produce intense light and heat, break windows, damage buildings, and throw people against nearby objects.\\\\n\\\\nScenes in schools, homes, streets, buses, and outdoor areas show children seeking cover quickly and staying down until the danger has passed. Viewers are told to enter a designated safe place after a warning, obey civil defense workers, avoid windows, and use walls, doorways, clothing, or even newspaper for protection when better cover is unavailable. The closing asks children to discuss emergency responses with their teachers and repeatedly answer the question of what to do after seeing a flash: \u201cDuck and cover.\u201d", "clips": [{"id": "1b034646-4e00-51b5-8a99-3447b1e8e743", "sourceSlug": "duck-and-cover", "sourceTitle": "Duck and Cover", "sourceYear": 1951, "position": 0, "startSeconds": 0.0, "endSeconds": 7.292, "videoUrl": "https://pub-075ff01374c04555b51c9bc50f258b42.r2.dev/sources/duck-and-cover/clips/0000000000-0000007292.mp4", "thumbnailUrl": "https://pub-075ff01374c04555b51c9bc50f258b42.r2.dev/sources/duck-and-cover/thumbnails/0000000000-0000007292.jpg"}, {"id": "1cefa623-c71a-59b3-be00-dec9d548ee26", "sourceSlug": "duck-and-cover", "sourceTitle": "Duck and Cover", "sourceYear": 1951, "position": 1, "startSeconds": 7.292, "endSeconds": 53.792, "videoUrl": "https://pub-075ff01374c04555b51c9bc50f258b42.r2.dev/sources/c5f621d6-6283-5f38-b6d8-e4185ce7c6b3/v5-windowed-clip-split-1/clips/1cefa623-c71a-59b3-be00-dec9d548ee26.mp4", "thumbnailUrl": "https://pub-075ff01374c04555b51c9bc50f258b42.r2.dev/sources/c5f621d6-6283-5f38-b6d8-e4185ce7c6b3/v5-windowed-clip-split-1/th